course: everyday
lesson: 75
title: The dentist and washing your hands
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dentist is a doctor for teeth: they look for holes, clean the teeth, and fix small problems before they become big and painful ones.
explain: Feeling nervous is normal; you can tell the dentist, ask what they will do, and agree a signal, like raising your hand, for them to stop.
explain: Germs are too small to see, and our hands carry them everywhere: onto food, into our mouths and onto things other people touch, so washing hands with soap is one of the best ways to stay well.
explain: Good washing reaches the parts people forget: the backs of the hands, between the fingers, the thumbs, the fingertips and the wrists.
explain: A girl in Denver cleans her rabbit's cage, then washes her hands with soap before she helps her dad make sandwiches, so no germs go from the rabbit into the food.
fact: Dentists explain each step and can stop if you raise your hand.
fact: A filling fixes a hole in a tooth so it does not get bigger and hurt more.
fact: After a numbing injection the lip and cheek feel fat and strange for a few hours, and you must not bite or chew them.
fact: Sealants and fluoride varnish are quick, painless coatings that help protect the back teeth from decay.
fact: With a brace you brush very carefully around the wires and avoid sticky and hard food.
fact: Hands are washed before eating or cooking, because dirty hands put germs on food.
fact: Good hand washing rubs the palms, the backs, between the fingers, the thumbs, the fingertips and the wrists.
fact: Pets can carry germs that make people ill, so hands are washed after touching them or their cages.
fact: After blowing your nose or coughing into your hands you wash or sanitise them (clean them with hand gel), so cold germs do not spread.
fact: If the soap at school runs out, you tell a teacher.
fact: Biting nails and sucking fingers move germs from the hands into the body.
exercise: You feel scared before going to the dentist. What can you do? | Tell the dentist you are nervous and ask what they will do | Keep very quiet the whole time and just hope it is over quickly | Refuse to open your mouth at all | Pretend you have no teeth problems
exercise: How can you let the dentist know you need a break? | Raise your hand, as you agreed | Bite the dentist's finger | Shout out as loudly as you possibly can | Kick your legs on the chair
exercise: The dentist says you need a filling. Why is it good to have it done? | It stops the hole from getting bigger and hurting more | It makes the tooth fall out sooner | It is just there to make the tooth look nicer and whiter | It turns a baby tooth into a grown-up one
exercise: After a numbing injection, why must you not chew on your lip? | You cannot feel pain there, so you could hurt it without noticing | The lip will stay numb for ever if you chew it while it is still numb | Chewing makes the injection stop working | The lip is too fat to fit between your teeth
exercise: What are sealants or fluoride varnish? | A quick painless coating that protects the back teeth | A kind of filling that needs drilling into the tooth first | A medicine you swallow | A brace for crooked teeth
exercise: You have a brace on your teeth. What should you be careful about? | Brushing around the wires and avoiding sticky and hard food | Never brushing near the brace in case the brush bends the wires | Eating only toffee and nuts | Taking the wires out at night
exercise: When should you wash your hands before food? | Before eating and before helping to cook | Only after eating | Only when they look dirty or feel sticky | Only before eating sweets
exercise: Which parts of your hands do people often forget to wash? | The backs, between the fingers, thumbs, fingertips and wrists | Only the palms, which hold the germs | Only the nails on the little fingers | Nothing, all parts get clean anyway
exercise: You just stroked a neighbour's dog. What should you do before eating? | Wash your hands | Wipe them on your trousers | Nothing, dogs are always clean | Lick your fingers clean
exercise: You coughed into your hands. What next? | Wash or sanitise your hands | Shake hands with a friend | Wipe them on the table | Nothing, coughs have no germs
exercise: The soap dispenser in the school toilets is empty. What should you do? | Tell a teacher | Rinse your hands in water and say nothing | Skip washing your hands every day | Use the floor cleaner instead
exercise: Why should you try to stop biting your nails? | It moves germs from your hands into your body | It makes your nails grow far too fast | It wears your teeth down into sharp points | It makes your fingers weaker
exercise: Why does food stuck in a brace matter? | It can cause decay around the wires | It makes the brace rusty | It makes the teeth straighter | It stops the brace from working at all
test: A boy in Leeds is nervous about the dentist. Which is true? | The dentist can explain each step and stop when he raises his hand | Dentists are not allowed to talk to children | He must keep quiet and never ask questions | Being nervous means he cannot be treated
test: After a filling, a girl's cheek feels huge and numb. What should she do at lunch? | Be careful not to bite her cheek or lip until the feeling returns | Chew hard on that side of her mouth to wake the numb feeling up again | Eat very hot soup to warm it | Nothing, numb places cannot be hurt
test: Why do dentists fix small holes early? | A small hole grows bigger and more painful if left | Small holes heal by themselves if they are left | Fillings only work on big holes | Dentists like to be busy
test: A girl with a brace wants caramel toffee. What would a dentist say? | Avoid it, it sticks to the brace and can break the wires | Eat as much as you like, because braces are strong enough for anything | Eat it only in the morning | Toffee helps straighten the teeth
test: A boy rinses only his palms quickly. What has he probably missed? | Germs between his fingers, on his thumbs and on his wrists | Nothing, the palms are where all the germs on our hands live | Only the germs on his elbows | Only clean parts that need no washing
test: Why do we wash hands after cleaning a hamster's cage? | Pets can carry germs that make people ill | Hamsters are poisonous to touch with bare hands | The cage makes your skin itchy | So the hamster does not smell us
test: What would probably happen if a child with a cold coughed into his hands and then opened the classroom door? | The next people to touch the handle could catch his germs | His cold would get better faster | The germs would all die on the cold metal door handle at once | Nothing, germs stay on the hands
test: Which habit helps germs get inside your body? | Sucking your fingers | Washing your hands before lunch | Using a tissue for your nose | Sneezing into your elbow
test: What does a sealant do to the back teeth? | Coats them to help stop decay | Pulls them out painlessly | Makes them grow bigger and stronger | Turns them white
test: dentist numbed my mouth, lip feels huge lol, can i eat a burger | Wait till it wears off so you do not bite your lip | Yes, eat now while it does not hurt, best time for it | Yes, chew on the numb side only | Yes, numb lips cannot be hurt
test: what if it hurts at the dentist and i want them to stop | Raise your hand and they can stop | Bite them so they know to stop | Shout and run out of the room as fast as u can | Nothing, you must stay quiet
test: just stroked the rabbit, can i have my sandwich | Wash your hands first, pets can carry germs | Yes, rabbits are always clean | Yes, just wipe your hands on a towel or your jeans | Only wash if it scratched you
test: soap's run out in the school loos again | Tell a teacher | Use just water and say nothing | Do not wash your hands at all | Take soap from another class
explain: A boy in Leeds skipped his dentist check-ups for two years; a small hole the dentist would have found early grew so big that the tooth needed a root treatment.
explain: At a birthday party in Ohio, several children who ate without washing their hands after playing outside got a tummy bug, and some were sick for days.
fact: Many tummy bugs spread when people do not wash their hands after the toilet or before eating.
fact: A dentist can find a small cavity before it hurts and fill it quickly; left alone, it grows and needs much bigger treatment.
test: teeth dont hurt so why go to the dentist | Small holes don't hurt yet, the dentist finds them before they grow | The dentist only helps when it hurts | Check-ups make teeth weaker | You only go if a tooth falls out
test: just came off the playground, gonna grab a sandwich | Wash your hands first, germs from outside can make you sick | Wipe your hands on your shirt, that's enough to clean them | Just blow on your hands | Germs can't live on hands outside
test: whole class got a tummy bug after the trip, why | Germs spread from unwashed hands to food and mouths | Tummy bugs come from cold weather | Too much running about on the trip gives you a tummy bug | Tummy bugs come from the bus seats only
test: omg did u hear half the kids at Zoe's party threw up for like a week cos nobody washed their hands, what's the lesson | Wash your hands with soap before eating and after the toilet | Parties are dangerous, never go to one | Only the cake made them sick, hands don't matter | Wash hands only if they look dirty
test: someone said the dentist drills right through ur head if u move, is that true lol | No, a rumour; the dentist explains each step and stops if you raise a hand | Yes, so you must never visit a dentist | Yes, but only for children under ten | Yes, dentists drill holes on purpose
test: everyone's sayin Max has disgusting hands and gave the whole class a tummy bug, should i spread it | No, don't blame or shame him; just wash your own hands well | Yes, everyone should stay away from him | Yes, but only tell the teacher and parents | Yes, and tell people he never washes his hands after the toilet
exam: Identify the principal function of a dental filling. | It repairs a cavity so that it does not enlarge and cause pain | It whitens the surface of the tooth permanently | It removes the tooth so that a new one may grow | It numbs the tooth so that it never feels pain again
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises thorough hand washing? | Rubbing the palms, backs, fingers, thumbs, fingertips and wrists | Rinsing the palms briefly in cold water without soap | Rubbing the palms alone for a few seconds | Wiping the hands upon one's clothing afterwards
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the condition of the lip subsequent to a numbing injection. | It feels swollen for some hours and must not be bitten or chewed | It is permanently numb and never regains its feeling | It may be chewed freely, since it cannot be harmed | It returns to normal sensation within one minute
exam: Determine the course of action required of a pupil upon discovering that the soap at school has been exhausted. | Inform a teacher | Abstain from washing for the remainder of term | Rinse with water alone and say nothing | Use washing-up liquid from the kitchen
